## Claude Code Workflow
  When working in this repository, prefer MCP filesystem tools over built-in editing flows.

  ## Tool Preference
  - `str_replace_based_edit_tool`: primary tool for edits to existing files (matches
    the tool schema Claude is natively trained to call: `view`/`str_replace`/`insert`)
  - `apply_patch`: alternative for multi-file or diff-style edits (Codex/OpenAI envelope)
  - `create_file` or `str_replace_based_edit_tool(command="create")`: for new files
  - `write_file`: only for full rewrites or when patching is not practical
  - `search_files` and `list_files`: for discovery
  - `read_file` or `str_replace_based_edit_tool(command="view")`: for targeted inspection

  ## Claude-Specific Guidance
  - Do not use `read_file` followed by `write_file` for small changes.
  - If you already have enough recent context, patch directly.
  - If a patch fails or the anchor is weak, read more context before retrying.
  - Prefer small, stable patches over large speculative edits.
  - `str_replace` requires `old_str` to match exactly once; include enough
    surrounding context to make it unique rather than retrying blindly.
  - If an edit turns out wrong, call `undo_edit` instead of re-patching over it.

  ## Edit Tool Usage

  ```text
  str_replace_based_edit_tool(command="view", path="path/to/file.py")

  str_replace_based_edit_tool(
      command="str_replace",
      path="path/to/file.py",
      old_str="old_value = 1",
      new_str="old_value = 2",
  )
  ```

  For multi-file or diff-shaped changes, Codex-style patch syntax via `apply_patch`
  also works:

  ```text
  *** Begin Patch
  *** Update File: path/to/file.py
  @@
  -old_value = 1
  +old_value = 2
  *** End Patch

  ## Verification

  After editing, use the project’s native checks when available:

  - syntax check
  - lint
  - targeted test
  - narrow regression check when justified